.TH NA_AUDIO_DAEMON 1 "2026-08-31" "naudio" "naudio manual"
.SH NAME
na_audio_daemon \- serve a real capture device as a naudio stream, or probe it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B na_audio_daemon
.RB [ \-\-mode
.IR capture\-probe | hardware | control ]
.RB [ \-\-capture
.IR pat \ |\ \-\-capture\-id
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-playback
.IR pat \ |\ \-\-playback\-id
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-transport
.IR tcp | udp | dual ]
.RB [ \-\-rate
.IR HZ ]
.RB [ \-\-channels
.IR 1 | 2 ]
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-duration\-ms
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file \ |\ \-\-no\-config ]
.br
.B na_audio_daemon \-\-mode control
.RB [ \-\-control\-port
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-autostart
.IR true | false ]
.RB [ \-\-open\-page ]
.br
.B na_audio_daemon \-\-list\-devices
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B na_audio_daemon
is the hardware driver of the naudio toolkit: it opens a
.I real
audio capture device (a radio's USB codec, a sound card, a microphone) and
either probes it directly or serves its audio as a naudio (net-audio
protocol) stream that any naudio client can receive \(em
.BR na_wav_tap (1),
the example play-to-speakers clients, or an application linking the naudio
library.
.PP
Three modes:
.TP
.B control
serve a control page on 127.0.0.1 and do nothing else until it is asked to.
Pick a capture device, change settings, start and stop the stream and watch it
run, from a browser \(em the way to operate the daemon without a terminal.
See
.B THE CONTROL PAGE
below.
.TP
.B capture\-probe
open the capture device directly and report frames, overflows and per-channel
RMS \(em the real-hardware capture gate, with no network involved.
Use it to prove capture works at all before adding a network hop.
.TP
.B hardware
(default) start a naudio server that captures the device and streams it;
an in-process RX-only client measures sustained throughput and RMS.
External clients can connect to the same server while it runs.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-capture " pat"
capture device name substring (default: \(lqUSB Audio CODEC\(rq).
.TP
.BI \-\-capture\-id " N"
force the capture device by backend id (skips the name lookup).
Ids come from
.BR \-\-list\-devices .
.TP
.BI \-\-playback " pat"
hardware mode: the client's RX sink device, e.g. a virtual device feeding a
digital-mode application.
When omitted \(em or when the pattern matches nothing \(em audio drains to a
hardware-free fake backend instead (see
.B CAVEATS
below).
.TP
.BI \-\-playback\-id " N"
force the playback device by backend id.
.TP
.BI \-\-transport " tcp\fR|\fPudp\fR|\fPdual"
server transport (default tcp).
.TP
.BI \-\-rate " HZ"
capture/server sample rate, 8000..192000 and divisible by 50 for an exact
20\ ms frame (default 48000).
naudio does
.B not
resample: the device must open at exactly this rate or the run refuses \(em
a silent fallback would ship wrong-rate audio labeled with this rate.
.TP
.BI \-\-channels " N"
1 (mono) or 2 (stereo, the default); refused on mismatch, like
.BR \-\-rate .
.TP
.BI \-\-port " N"
server port; 0 picks an ephemeral port (default 4533).
.TP
.BI \-\-duration\-ms " N"
run time in milliseconds; 0 runs until Ctrl-C (default 30000).
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
read settings from
.I file
(see
.B CONFIGURATION FILE
below) instead of the default location.
The file must exist.
Flags given on the same command line still override it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
read no configuration file at all, not even the default one.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-devices
enumerate capture and playback devices with their backend ids, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
print usage and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-control\-port " N"
.RB ( control
mode) the port the control page listens on, 1\(en65535; the default is 8737.
0 asks the operating system for a free port, which prints a different URL at
every start and is therefore a poor choice for a service.
.TP
.BI \-\-autostart " true" | false
.RB ( control
mode) start capturing as soon as the daemon starts, rather than waiting for
the page's Start button.
This is how \(lqstream at every login\(rq is expressed: the service itself
stays inert until you set it.
Default
.BR false .
.TP
.B \-\-open\-page
.RB ( control
mode) open the control page in the default browser at startup.
Used by the installed shortcuts; it is an error in any other mode, because
there would be no page to open.
.SH THE CONTROL PAGE
.B \-\-mode control
starts a small web server on
.I 127.0.0.1
and nothing else: no device is opened until you press
.B Start
on the page (or set
.BR autostart ).
The page is where the whole configuration surface lives \(em a device picker
built from the same enumeration
.B \-\-list\-devices
reports, every setting in this manual as a form field, and Start / Stop /
Restart / Quit for the stream, with the delivered percentage, connected
clients, per-channel levels and error counters updating as it runs.
Saving writes the configuration file described below, so what you set on the
page is what the service reads at your next login.
.PP
The installed packages create a shortcut that opens it: a
.B naudio Control
entry in the Windows Start menu, a
.B naudio Control
desktop entry on Linux, and
.I /Applications/naudio Control.app
on macOS, where a fresh install lists the background service described below
under the same name in
.IR "System Settings > General > Login Items & Extensions" ;
a Mac that already had the service from an earlier release keeps listing it
under the developer's name, because macOS names that entry once, when it first
sees the service, and neither reinstalling nor restarting renames it.
All three run the same command, and using one twice is safe \(em if a control
page is already answering on the configured port, the second launch opens that
page and exits rather than failing to bind.
By hand, the equivalent is:
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_audio_daemon \-\-mode control \-\-duration\-ms 0 \-\-open\-page
.RE
.fi
.SS What it is not
The page binds
.I 127.0.0.1
only and is not reachable from the network; naudio has no remote management,
by design.
There is no password: any program running as any user on this machine can
reach it, which on a shared machine is worth knowing, though such a program
could open the audio device directly in any case.
.PP
What
.I is
defended is your browser, because a page on the open internet can make a
browser send requests to your own machine.
Requests are refused unless they name this host, come from this page's own
origin, and \(em for anything that changes something \(em arrive as
.IR application/json ,
which an HTML form cannot produce.
.SS Rescanning devices
The device list can only be refreshed while the stream is
.IR stopped :
PortAudio cannot be re-enumerated safely underneath a run that is opening
streams on another thread.
A device plugged in while streaming appears after you stop.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
Every option above that takes a value can also be set from a configuration
file, one
.B key = value
per line; the key is the option's name without its leading dashes:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# the radio's codec, served over UDP until stopped
capture = USB Audio CODEC
transport = udp
port = 4533
duration\-ms = 0
.RE
.fi
.PP
Lines starting with
.B #
are comments.
Whitespace around keys and values is trimmed; spaces
.I inside
a value are kept, so device patterns need no quoting.
Command-line flags always override the file.
.PP
Unless
.B \-\-config
or
.B \-\-no\-config
is given, the daemon reads the platform's conventional location when the
file exists (and simply uses its defaults when it does not):
.TP
macOS
.I ~/Library/Application\ Support/naudio/daemon.conf
.TP
Windows
.I %ProgramData%\enaudio\edaemon.conf
.TP
other systems
.I /etc/naudio/daemon.conf
.PP
Parsing is strict: an unknown key, a malformed line, or an out-of-range
value stops the daemon with the file name and line number rather than
being skipped \(em a typo'd key silently ignored would run the daemon on
the wrong device or port while looking configured.
.B \-\-list\-devices,
.B \-\-help
and
.B \-\-open\-page
are actions, not settings, and have no key.
.PP
The control page writes this file when you save (see
.B THE CONTROL PAGE
above), so it is not a file you have to edit by hand.
It is rewritten whole each time, which means comments you add are lost \(em
edit it in one place or the other, not both.
Only settings that differ from their defaults are written: a file listing
every default would freeze them, so a later naudio that changed one would be
silently overridden by a value you never chose.
.SH SERVICE REGISTRATION
The packages install a service definition so the daemon can run without a
terminal.
Installing a package must not start capturing a microphone at your next
login, and on no platform does it: the service runs
.B \-\-mode control
and opens no device until you press Start on the page.
Where the platforms differ is the switch.
.PP
On macOS the installer registers a launchd
.I agent
at
.IR /Library/LaunchAgents/org.kj5hst.naudio.daemon.plist ,
and the switch is the system's: the service is listed in
.I "System Settings > General > Login Items & Extensions > Allow in the Background"
as
.BR "naudio Control" ,
on.
From your next login the daemon is running and its page is a click away;
turning the switch off there stops it and keeps it off, turning it on starts
it again.
There is no command to run.
Earlier releases named
.B launchctl enable
and
.B disable
as the switch; they do not move the Login Items switch (the two are
independent stores), so the installer clears a
.B disable
you may have run.
.PP
On Linux the service arrives
.I inert
and you enable it once, deliberately: the packages install a systemd
.I user
unit at
.IR /usr/lib/systemd/user/naudio\-daemon.service :
.PP
.nf
.RS
systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now naudio\-daemon
.RE
.fi
.PP
Both are session-scoped rather than system services, because capturing audio
is a session privilege: a root daemon can never be granted a microphone by
macOS, and a system-scope unit cannot reach your PipeWire or PulseAudio
socket.
All three pass
.B \-\-duration\-ms 0
on the command line, so the service runs until it is stopped no matter what
the configuration file says; every other setting comes from that file.
.PP
On Windows the installer registers a Task Scheduler
.I logon task
named
.IR \(rsnaudio\(rsnaudio\-daemon .
Enable it from Task Scheduler, or with:
.PP
.nf
.RS
schtasks /Change /TN "\(rsnaudio\(rsnaudio\-daemon" /ENABLE
schtasks /Run    /TN "\(rsnaudio\(rsnaudio\-daemon"
.RE
.fi
.PP
It is a logon task and deliberately not a Windows service: services run in
session 0, which has no audio endpoints, so one would install cleanly and then
capture nothing.
Unlike the other two platforms, Task Scheduler does not redirect a task's
output anywhere, so there is no log file to read \(em a configuration error
shows as
.B LastTaskResult
2 on the task, and on the control page.
.PP
All three units run
.BR "\-\-mode control" ,
so a service that is on gives you the control page at every login and captures
nothing until you ask it to.
Set
.B autostart
in the configuration file (the checkbox on the page) if you want capture to
begin automatically instead.
.SS Changing the configuration
The daemon reads its configuration file once, at startup.
There is no reload signal, and a change of sample rate, channel count or
transport could not be applied to a running stream anyway.
.I Restarting the service is the way to apply a change:
.PP
.nf
.RS
launchctl kickstart \-k gui/$(id \-u)/org.kj5hst.naudio.daemon
systemctl \-\-user restart naudio\-daemon
schtasks /End /TN "\(rsnaudio\(rsnaudio\-daemon" & schtasks /Run /TN "\(rsnaudio\(rsnaudio\-daemon"
.RE
.fi
.PP
The control page's
.B Restart
button does the same thing for the stream without restarting the daemon, which
is the usual way to apply a change made on the page.
.SS When the service will not start
A configuration error stops the daemon with exit status 2 and a
.IR file : line
message naming the offending line.
The systemd unit treats that status as permanent and stops retrying, leaving
the unit
.B failed
with the message visible in
.BR "systemctl \-\-user status naudio\-daemon" .
launchd cannot distinguish exit statuses, so the agent instead retries every
30 seconds and writes the same message to
.IR /usr/local/var/log/naudio/daemon.log ,
which is the file to read when a Mac service will not come up.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
the probe completed with no overruns, or the hardware run streamed with no
errors.
.TP
.B 1
a runtime failure: device open refused, overruns, stream errors.
.TP
.B 2
usage error.
.SH CAVEATS
A
.B \-\-playback
pattern that matches
.I nothing
falls back to the same hardware-free drain as omitting the option, and the
run can still exit 0 \(em so a misspelled sink name reports a PASS for a
check that never fed your application.
Confirm the \(lqclient sink\(rq line in the output names a real device
before trusting a hardware-mode pass.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find the radio's audio device:
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_audio_daemon \-\-list\-devices
.RE
.fi
.PP
Prove capture works, no network involved:
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_audio_daemon \-\-mode capture\-probe \-\-capture\-id 3 \-\-duration\-ms 30000
.RE
.fi
.PP
Serve the device until interrupted, for remote clients:
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_audio_daemon \-\-mode hardware \-\-capture\-id 3 \-\-transport tcp \-\-duration\-ms 0
.RE
.fi
.PP
Provision a constrained link at 12\ kHz mono (192\ kbps on the wire instead
of the default 1.536\ Mbps):
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_audio_daemon \-\-mode hardware \-\-capture\-id 3 \-\-rate 12000 \-\-channels 1
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR na_wav_tap (1),
.BR na_hamlib_bridge (1)
.PP
The full on-air verification procedure:
.I docs/on\-air\-verification.md
in the naudio source tree
(https://github.com/KJ5HST\-LABS/naudio).
